= Abstract
The computer mouse, a cornerstone of modern human-computer interaction, has remained largely unchanged since its invention. In this paper, we introduce the Computer Hamster, a playful reimagining of the traditional pointing device: a truly wireless mouse. By blending biological metaphor with technological innovation, we explore how a small, furry hamster-inspired design can replace cumbersome cables while retaining precise cursor control. Prototypes demonstrate that, while the Computer Hamster does not require batteries or Wi-Fi, it embodies the spirit of freedom, agility, and unpredictability long associated with its silicon-and-plastic predecessors. Through this whimsical approach, we argue that the evolution from “mouse” to “hamster” not only resolves practical connectivity issues but also invites users to reconsider the literal and figurative boundaries of interface devices.